{% extends 'admin/_main/layout.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}{{ rfq ? 'Edit ' ~ rfq.documentNumber : 'New RFQ' }} | Admin{% endblock %}

{#
    The requirement form.

    Built against the SELL side's quote form (templates/admin/estimate/form.html.twig) — the
    workspace shell, the info panel, the address panel, the line table and the footer action row all
    come from there — because that is the standard both sides of this application are converging on.
    It is deliberately NOT a copy of the purchase order form, which is a sketch of the same kind this
    screen used to be, and copying it is how the thinness spread in the first place.

    ## What this form does NOT have, and must never grow

    No price column. No tax code. No currency. No totals box.

    An RFQ names a REQUIREMENT put to several vendors at once: what is needed, how much, where it
    goes. It has no single counterparty, so there is nothing to denominate a price in and nobody to
    owe it to — the argued exception in `EveryDocumentDeclaresItsContractTest::NOT_COMMERCIAL`, and
    still correct. The money belongs to each vendor's own reply, which is a separate document with a
    separate screen (`rfq_vendor_reply_edit.html.twig`), one per vendor, priced independently. That
    separation IS the feature: it is what makes "four competing quotes against one requirement" a
    thing the database can say rather than a spreadsheet somebody keeps beside it.

    Wanting a price on this page is the signal that the two documents have been merged again.

    ## No JavaScript anywhere on it

    The bundle ships no add-a-row script, so the spare rows below are always visible and "Save & add
    more lines" is a real submit that lands back here with fresh ones. Remove is a real submit too,
    carrying its own row index. See RfqController::save().
#}

{% block admin_body %}
    {% include '@Procurement/_nav.html.twig' with {
        current: 'rfqs',
        heading: rfq ? 'Edit ' ~ rfq.documentNumber : 'New RFQ',
        lead: 'What is needed, how much, and where it goes — vendor-agnostic. Prices belong to each vendor\'s reply, not to this document.'
    } only %}

    <section class="order-create-shell">
        <form method="post" id="rfq-form" class="order-workspace-card" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_rfq_save') }}">
            {{ csrf_field() }}
            <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ rfq ? rfq.id : 0 }}">

            <div class="order-workspace-grid">
                <section class="order-form-panel">
                    <h2>Requirement Info</h2>
                    <div class="order-field-row static">
                        <label>RFQ #:</label>
                        <strong>{{ rfq ? rfq.documentNumber : 'Allocated when this is first saved' }}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="order-field-row static">
                        <label>Status:</label>
                        <strong>{% include 'admin/_partials/document_status.html.twig' with {status: rfq ? rfq.status.value : 'Draft', loud: false} only %}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="order-field-row static">
                        <label>Raised:</label>
                        <strong>{{ rfq ? rfq.createdAt|date('Y-m-d') : 'on first save' }}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <label class="order-field-row">
                        <span>Deliver to warehouse:</span>
                        <select name="warehouse_id">
                            <option value="0">&mdash; choose a warehouse &mdash;</option>
                            {% for warehouse in warehouses %}
                                {# The address, not just the name (queue item 37) — a warehouse has one now, and
                                   which site the goods go to is the whole question this select answers. #}
                                <option value="{{ warehouse.id }}"{{ rfq and rfq.warehouse and rfq.warehouse.id == warehouse.id ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ warehouse.name }}{% if warehouse.addressSummary %} — {{ warehouse.addressSummary }}{% endif %}</option>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </select>
                    </label>
                    <p class="field-hint">
                        Required before this can be tendered — receiving has to put stock in a real warehouse, and
                        the purchase order raised from the winning reply inherits it.
                    </p>
                    <label class="order-field-row">
                        <span>Notes to vendors:</span>
                        <textarea name="notes" rows="3" placeholder="Lead time expectations, packaging, who to quote to">{{ rfq ? rfq.notes : '' }}</textarea>
                    </label>
                </section>

                {# ── Deliver To ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                   The quote form's address panels, in the one form this document can carry them.

                   CORRECTION (queue item 37). This comment used to say "a `warehouse` row in this
                   application is a name and a status — there is no address on it to print", which
                   stopped being true the moment queue item 32 gave `App\Entity\Warehouse` a real
                   address; the same false sentence was carried in `Rfq::$deliveryAddress` and is
                   corrected there too. The site the goods would go to IS sayable now, and the
                   select above says it.

                   This field still earns its place, for the reason the comment should always have
                   given: a tender may state a delivery point that is NOT one of our warehouses — a
                   customer's dock on a drop-ship, a bonded site, a third-party cross-dock — and
                   every vendor is quoting freight against whatever is written here, which is most
                   of the difference between two quotes for the same goods. Free text rather than a
                   structured address: this is our own site, typed once, read by a human at the
                   vendor, and it is never matched or geocoded the way a customer's shipping
                   address is. #}
                <section class="order-form-panel">
                    <h2>Deliver To</h2>
                    <label class="order-field-row">
                        <span>Delivery address:</span>
                        <textarea name="delivery_address" rows="6" placeholder="Street, city, province, postal code, receiving hours">{{ rfq ? rfq.deliveryAddress : '' }}</textarea>
                    </label>
                    <p class="field-hint">
                        Left blank, the tender and every reply printed from it fall back to this company's own
                        address, below — from Settings &rsaquo; Company Information. Fill this in when the goods go
                        somewhere other than head office, or when the vendor needs receiving hours and a door.
                    </p>
                    <div class="order-field-row static">
                        <label>Company address:</label>
                        <strong>
                            {{ app_setting('company_name', '') ?: 'No company name set' }}<br>
                            {{ app_setting('company_address', '') }}<br>
                            {{ [app_setting('company_city', ''), province_name(app_setting('company_country', ''), app_setting('company_state', ''))]|filter(v => v)|join(', ') }}
                            {{ app_setting('company_postal_code', '') }}
                        </strong>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {# ── Why there is no price on this page ───────────────────────────────────────
                   Stated on the screen and not only in the code, because the assumption runs the
                   other way: every other document in this bundle has a total on it, and an admin
                   who has just come from the purchase order form has every reason to look for the
                   cost column here and conclude it is missing. #}
                <section class="order-form-panel">
                    <h2>Prices</h2>
                    <div class="quote-stock-notice">
                        <p><strong>An RFQ carries no prices, and that is deliberate.</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>This document asks SEVERAL vendors the same question, so it has no counterparty, no currency and no total of its own.</li>
                            <li>Each vendor's answer is its own document — one reply per vendor, priced independently, with its own subtotal, tax and total.</li>
                            <li>Send this requirement out, then record what each vendor quotes on that vendor's reply screen.</li>
                            <li>Accepting one reply raises the purchase order and marks every other reply as not taken; nothing is deleted.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>
            </div>

            <section class="order-lines-section">
                <h2>Requirement lines</h2>
                <p class="field-hint">
                    A row becomes a line when it names something and asks for more than zero. Blank rows are
                    ignored, so the spares below cost nothing.
                </p>

                <div class="wide-table-wrap">
                    <table class="wide-price-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Product</th><th>Description</th><th>SKU</th><th>Quantity</th><th>Line notes</th><th>Actions</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            {% set existing = rfq ? rfq.lines|length : 0 %}
                            {% for line in (rfq ? rfq.lines : []) %}
                                <tr>
                                    <td data-label="Product">{{ include('@Procurement/_product_field.html.twig', {
                                        field: 'lines[' ~ loop.index0 ~ '][product_id]',
                                        products: productOptions,
                                        remote: productsRemote,
                                        selected: line.product,
                                        vendorId: null,
                                        label: 'Product for line ' ~ (loop.index0 + 1),
                                    }) }}</td>
                                    <td data-label="Description"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ loop.index0 }}][name]" value="{{ line.name }}" maxlength="255" aria-label="Description for line {{ loop.index0 + 1 }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="SKU"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ loop.index0 }}][sku]" value="{{ line.sku }}" maxlength="80" aria-label="SKU for line {{ loop.index0 + 1 }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="Quantity"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ loop.index0 }}][quantity]" value="{{ line.quantity }}" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Quantity for line {{ loop.index0 + 1 }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="Line notes"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ loop.index0 }}][notes]" value="{{ line.notes }}" aria-label="Notes for line {{ loop.index0 + 1 }}"></td>
                                    {# A real submit, not a scripted one: this bundle ships no JS for
                                       these tables, and a line table you can only add to is not an
                                       editable line table. The value is the row index the server
                                       drops — see RfqController::save(). #}
                                    <td data-label="Actions">
                                        <button class="table-action danger" type="submit" name="remove_line" value="{{ loop.index0 }}">Remove</button>
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            {% endfor %}
                            {% for blank in 1..3 %}
                                {% set index = existing + blank - 1 %}
                                <tr>
                                    <td data-label="Product">{{ include('@Procurement/_product_field.html.twig', {
                                        field: 'lines[' ~ index ~ '][product_id]',
                                        products: productOptions,
                                        remote: productsRemote,
                                        selected: null,
                                        vendorId: null,
                                        label: 'Product for new line ' ~ blank,
                                    }) }}</td>
                                    <td data-label="Description"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ index }}][name]" maxlength="255" aria-label="Description for new line {{ blank }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="SKU"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ index }}][sku]" maxlength="80" aria-label="SKU for new line {{ blank }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="Quantity"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ index }}][quantity]" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Quantity for new line {{ blank }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="Line notes"><input type="text" name="lines[{{ index }}][notes]" aria-label="Notes for new line {{ blank }}"></td>
                                    <td data-label="Actions">&mdash;</td>
                                </tr>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="order-lines-footer">
                    {# The no-JS way to add a fourth, fifth, sixth line: save what is typed and come
                       back here with three fresh spares. The estimate form's bottom bar does the
                       same thing with the same button pair, for the same reason. #}
                    <button class="button" type="submit" name="action" value="save_continue">Save &amp; add more lines</button>
                </div>
            </section>

            <div class="form-actions">
                <button class="button primary" type="submit">Save draft</button>
                <a class="button back-button" href="{{ rfq ? path('admin_bundle_procurement_rfq', {id: rfq.id}) : path('admin_bundle_procurement_rfqs') }}">Back</a>
            </div>
        </form>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
